# Skill — Hill-Climb Loop

> Install as `.github/skills/hill-climb/SKILL.md` (cross-surface: cloud agent, CLI, IDEs).

## When to invoke

- The task has the shape **"iterate on X with a profiler/measurement and a dataset until it hits Y"**:
  cold-launch time, app size, build wall-clock, test-suite duration, memory at a percentile, frame
  rate, cost per unit of work.
- A number exists (or can be built in one session) that a command can print deterministically.

## When NOT to invoke

- No measurable Y ("make it feel faster") — first build the measurement, then hill-climb.
- The metric is a proxy you have not verified reflects the real goal (Pitfall 25 — verify the
  reader sees the whole set before trusting its number).
- Feature work wearing a metric costume.

## Inputs (all required before iteration 1)

- **Metric X** — one number, one direction. `<METRIC_DEFINITION>`
- **Measure command** — `<COMMAND_THAT_PRINTS_X>`, deterministic enough that two consecutive runs
  agree within `<TOLERANCE>`; if it doesn't, fix the harness first (median of N runs, pinned
  dataset, warm/cold stated).
- **Dataset** — the fixed workload the measurement runs against. Pin it (a commit, a fixture set, a
  recorded trace). A moving dataset makes every comparison a lie.
- **Target Y** — the stop-when-reached number, set by a human.
- **Budget** — max iterations / wall-clock / tokens for this climb.

## Workflow

### 1. Baseline

Run the measure command. Record iteration 0 in the ratchet file (below). No baseline, no climb.

### 2. One hypothesis, one change

Name the suspected bottleneck ("what would prove me wrong: the profile shows <X> is not where the
time goes"). Make the smallest change that tests it. One variable per iteration — batched changes
make the measurement unattributable.

### 3. Measure again

Same command, same dataset. Improved → keep, commit, append to the ratchet. Regressed or flat →
revert *fully*, append the negative result anyway (a failed hypothesis is evidence the next
iteration needs).

### 4. Repeat until a stop condition

- **Target hit** — report, with the ratchet file as the proof trail.
- **`<N_FLAT>` consecutive non-improving iterations** — report a plateau + the best remaining
  hypotheses; a human decides whether to re-budget.
- **Budget exhausted** — report where the climb stood.

## The ratchet file

`docs/hill-climbs/<METRIC_SLUG>.md`, append-only:

```
| iter | date | change (one line) | X before | X after | kept? |
|------|------|-------------------|----------|---------|-------|
| 0    | <D>  | baseline          | —        | <X0>    | —     |
| 1    | <D>  | <CHANGE>          | <X0>     | <X1>    | yes   |
```

The ratchet is the claim: any "we improved X" statement cites it. It also stops the next session
from re-trying reverted hypotheses (append-only failure notes are half its value).

## Evidence rules

- The measure command is the only truth — never report an improvement from reasoning, a diff, or a
  partial run.
- Ratchet rows are written at measurement time, not reconstructed later.
- A regression shipped while climbing a different metric is a finding, not collateral — report it.

## Handing off

A hill-climb pairs naturally with a standing routine (`docs/14-STANDING-ROUTINES.md`): the routine
runs one bounded iteration per scheduled run, the ratchet file carries state between runs, and the
routine's PR-and-review gates apply to every kept change.
